.TH CHTTPCLIENT_DO_ASYNC_STREAMING 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpclient_do_async_streaming \- submit an HTTP request for asynchronous execution, and stream the response body
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

ctpool_future *chttpclient_do_async_streaming(chttpcli cli,
                                               const chttp_request_t *req,
                                               chttpcli_write_fn write_fn,
                                               void *write_ctx);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
The submit works as in chttpclient_do_async and does not block, but
the client calls write_fn one or more times with chunks of the
response body as they arrive over the wire, as chttpclient_do_streaming
does.

write_fn runs on one of the engine's reactor threads: NOT on the
caller's thread, and NOT on a thread of its own for this request.
Unlike the callback of chttpclient_do_streaming, which runs on the
caller's thread, this one has two hard rules. First, write_fn must not
block. It must do no blocking I/O, must not hold a lock for long and
must not wait on the future of another request, because blocking there
stalls every other connection that the engine multiplexes on that
reactor thread. Second, write_fn must not call chttpclient_do_async or
chttpclient_do_async_streaming again, and must not call anything that
waits, directly or indirectly, on the future of this same request.
Both rules apply to the same chttpcli and to any other chttpcli that
shares the engine, since such a call can deadlock against the reactor
thread that runs write_fn.

No call to write_fn starts after the future is fulfilled, so the caller
may free write_ctx once it has the result. If the request timeout
passes while write_fn is running, the client waits for that call to
return before it fulfils the future with ccol_timed_out, and passes no
further chunk to write_fn.

Calling chttpclient_destroy on any client from inside write_fn is a
fatal error. For cli the destroy would wait for the request that runs
it, and for another client it would wait for work that the engine
threads run, including the thread that runs write_fn; see
chttpclient_destroy(3). chttpcli_engine_wait from inside write_fn is
fatal for the same reason.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cli       The client handle. It must not be CHTTPCLI_INVALID.
req       The request to run. It must not be NULL. Unlike with
          chttpclient_do_streaming, req does not have to stay valid
          after this call returns, because the library copies
          everything it needs before returning.
write_fn  The callback that receives each chunk. It must not be NULL.
write_ctx An opaque context pointer that the client passes to
          write_fn unchanged.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A future, or NULL under the same conditions as chttpclient_do_async
plus one more, a NULL write_fn. After a successful submit, the result
of the future holds ccol_http_transfer_aborted if write_fn ever returns
anything other than len, since any such value, above len or below it,
aborts the transfer. The result never holds ccol_msg_too_large,
because chttpclient_set_max_response_body_size has no effect on a
streaming request.
.fi
